WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.300
Good morning John!

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.150
So I have a head cold, which I would complain
about, except that you just got home from

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.309
the hospital 'cause you have viral meningitis.
Which is maybe a little bit worse than a cold,

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:13.190
uhh, so I feel like I should suck it up and
just do my best to entertain you and make

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.860
you feel better while your head is trying
to fall off of your body, so here's a monkey

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.780
selfie! Isn't that a cute monkey?

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.760
This picture was taken when photographer David
Slater's camera was stolen by a Rare Crested

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.700
Black Macaque. That macaque then proceeded
to take lots and lots of pictures of itself,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.660
of other macaques, of just... leaves. Most
of them were terrible - like anybody with

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.120
a DSLR.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.560
But this one was very good, and not only because
the macaque has the beautiful smile on its

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.940
face and he's in focus and there's a nice
depth of field thing going on and it's good

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.130
composition, but also because the idea of
selfies has sort of permutated culture and

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.300
is now a thing.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.420
And the idea of a monkey taking a selfie is
kind of silly and funny and people want to

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.070
put that on their blogs. All of this has combined
into this become David Slater's most famous

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:00.660
photograph ever - most successful photograph
ever. At least, by some measures.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.220
The problem is that David Slater makes his
living by selling photographs, but this picture

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.280
has been declared by the Wikimedia Foundation
as being in the public domain, meaning it's

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.210
free for everyone to use.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.720
Why? This is David Slater's photograph - he
should have the ability to sell it.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.720
Well... he didn't take it. It was taken by
a monkey, and monkeys, at least so far, are

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.799
not legally able to own things, anything,
including intellectual property.

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:32.380
This is unfortunate for David Slater who carried
lots of very heavy and expensive equipment

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.280
a very long way to get this photograph, but
it seems to me like the Wikimedia Foundation's

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:41.960
argument makes sense. It's everybody's photo,
which is why I can use it in this video, because

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.310
macaques can't own stuff.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.570
Anyhow, viral meningitis! Woah... huh!?

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:51.409
If you're confused, John, who is the other
half of this video blog --if you're very confused

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:57.159
-- is my brother and he got viral meningitis
last week. He's home from the hospital now,

00:01:57.159 --> 00:01:59.409
he's fine, although he's still in a significant
amount of pain.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:04.700
This has resulted in a first-ever-of-all-time
thing on Vlogbrothers: I have three videos

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.289
in a row on this channel... because Esther
Day happened as well, and John missed a video,

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.770
and so now... Wow... it's like the Hank Show...
I don't like it.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.230
Please, John, come back, because I don't know
what to talk about without riffing off of

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:16.260
you.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.590
John has now missed three videos because of
hospitalizations...

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.470
Once for his orbital cellulitis - when his,
the back of his eyeball got infected. Once

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:29.620
for his gallbladder being removed, and now
for viral meningitis. Stop, John, stop.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.830
That first time John missed his video, when
he had the orbital cellulitis, the eye infection,

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.910
we banded together and we made a nice video
to help him feel better -- people put stuff

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.370
on their heads for him. Because putting stuff
on your head is funny and it makes people

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.480
feel better. It's a well-known scientific
fact... uh there's journal articles all about

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:45.819
it. Oooh!

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:49.540
So I'm gonna do it again and, in the spirit
of questionable copyright, I'm gonna do it

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.349
with the help of my friend Calvin Harris.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:55.319
[singing]
Meningitis is a bummer,

00:02:55.319 --> 00:03:02.319
When your head starts to pound,
The stiffness in the neck and

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.140
the sensitivity to sound,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:15.140
Rising pressure in your cranium,
and your blood pressure down,

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:22.590
Your brain gets starved for oxygen now,
but you will rebound,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.750
Meningitis is a bummer

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.000
John, I really, really, really, really, really
hope that I'll see you on Tuesday, uh, 'cause

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:52.690
I don't wanna do this by myself anymore.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.389
It's not Vlogbrother!!

